<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leitha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Leitha is a river about 180 kilometres long that flows from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, where it joins the Moson arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. For much of its course it formed the historical border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Hungary. After 1867 the two halves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> were informally called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisleithania">Cisleithania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Transleithania</a> after it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leitha.jpg" alt="Leitha" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Glock · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leitha_Kleinwolkersdorf.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
